  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 206 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nBy Friday night, the bulk of convection moves north and east beyond \nour area. Behind the upper-level system Saturday morning and \nafternoon, some remnant isolated showers will be possible across the \narea. The relatively better chances will be across our far western \ncounties and in/near the higher terrain (10-30% chances there). \nNevertheless, given the isolated nature of this convection, most \nlocations still look to remain dry for Halloween. By Sunday, the \nactive weather pattern continues as a pair of upper-level troughs \napproach west Texas and southeast New Mexico from the west and \nnorthwest. Ahead of this, shower and thunderstorm chances increase \nagain, this time mainly across southeast New Mexico and the northern \nPermian Basin. Chances range from 20-40% in general for these \nlocations.\n\nMonday and Tuesday, the aforementioned troughs (which at this time \nwill have merged together) move across the region. By Monday \nafternoon, most moisture will have been pushed east of our area. \nThat keeps rain chances at a minimum for most locations as the upper \nlow passes. The exception will be in the Lower Trans-Pecos and far \neastern Permian Basin, where remnant moisture allows low (20-40%) \nshower and thunderstorm chances to hang around through Monday \nevening. As the system moves across the region, an attendant upper-\nlevel jet moves overhead, while a nearby surface low pushes a cold \nfront into our area. Both of these features imply windy conditions \nMonday and Tuesday across the vast majority of west Texas and \nsoutheast New Mexico, but it is too early to tell if/where wind \nproducts will be necessary (though this pattern certainly does favor \nat least high winds in the Guadalupes). Otherwise temperatures cool \nbehind the front. Highs Saturday through Monday largely top out in \nthe mid-to-upper 70s and low 80s. By Tuesday and Wednesday, plenty \nof spread still exists within guidance as to just how much cooler we \nget, but right now it looks like most locations will see highs in \nthe upper 60s and low 70s (near-to-below seasonal norms for early \nNovember).\n\nSprang",
